['Here's a basic implementation for such a logging utility in C, using POSIX threads (pthreads) for thread safety and asynchronous I/O for non-blocking writes. The code doesn't contain any actual file rotation or disk space checks but it shows the structure you could follow to implement these features:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdarg.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <fcntl.h>

typedef enum { DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR } LogLevel;

typedef struct {
    char *message;
    LogLevel level;
} log_record;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
int fd; // File descriptor for logging file

void write_log(LogLevel level, const char *fmt, ...) {
    va_list args;
    pthread_cleanup_push((void(*)(void*))punlock, &lock);
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    va_start(args, fmt);
    log_record *rec = malloc(sizeof(log_record));
    rec->level = level;
    int length = vsnprintf(NULL, 0, fmt, args) + 1; // Get string size
    rec->message = malloc(length);
    vsprintf(rec->message, fmt, args);

    write(fd, rec->message, strlen(rec->message));

    free(rec->message);
    free(rec);

    va_end(args);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    pthread_cleanup_pop(0);
}

void async_write_log(LogLevel level, const char *fmt, ...) {
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, fmt);

    log_record *rec = malloc(sizeof(log_record));
    rec->level = level;
    int length = vsnprintf(NULL, 0, fmt, args) + 1; // Get string size
    rec->message = malloc(length);
    vsprintf(rec->message, fmt, args);

    aio_write(&aiocb);

    va_end(args);
}

void initialize() {
    fd = open("log.txt", O_CREAT | O_WRONLY, 0644); // Open file for logging
}

int main() {
    initialize();
    write_log(INFO, "Hello world!\n");
    return 0;
}
```

This example is a very basic and simplified version of what you're asking for. For real-world use, more features need to be added:

1. Error handling should be implemented using errno and error codes should be used instead of simple asserts.
2. The logging system can be expanded with additional functionalities like log filters, custom formatters, etc.
3. Log rotation needs a separate thread to periodically check the log file size, rotate it if needed, etc.
4. Throttling mechanism could use atomic counters and conditions for signalling when the system is below or above limits.
5. Integration with external systems can be done using APIs (if available), otherwise using a message queue service might be an option.
6. Documentation will have to be written in detail, covering installation guides, configuration instructions, etc.
7. Test coverage should include unit tests for individual functionalities and integration tests for the whole system. This can be done with frameworks like CUnit or CppUTest.
8. For scalability, we need to consider load balancing (if our app is distributed), horizontal scaling (adding more machines) etc. Reliability would require proper error handling and recovery mechanisms. Maintainability can be achieved through modular design, clear naming conventions, comments etc.
']

['This is a complex task that requires deep understanding of cryptography and security standards and advanced programming skills. It's impossible to write the entire code block here, but I can give you some skeleton codes for DES and RSA encryption.

For DES encryption:
```cpp
#include <openssl/des.h>

void encrypt(DES_cblock key, DES_cblock ivec, const char* input, char* output) {
    DES_key_schedule schedule;
    DES_set_key_unchecked(&key, &schedule);
    DES_ede3_cbc_encrypt((const unsigned char*)input, (unsigned char*)output, strlen(input), &schedule, &ivec, DES_ENCRYPT);
}

void decrypt(DES_cblock key, DES_cblock ivec, const char* input, char* output) {
    DES_key_schedule schedule;
    DES_set_key_unchecked(&key, &schedule);
    DES_ede3_cbc_encrypt((const unsigned char*)input, (unsigned char*)output, strlen(input), &schedule, &ivec, DES_DECRYPT);
}
```
For RSA encryption:
```cpp
#include <openssl/rsa.h>

RSA* createRSA(unsigned char * key,int public)
{
    RSA *rsa= NULL;
    BIO *keybio ;
    keybio = BIO_new_mem_buf(key, -1);
    if (keybio==NULL)
    {
        printf( "Failed to create key BIO" );
        return 0;
    }
    if(public)
    {
        rsa = PEM_read_bio_RSA_PUBKEY(keybio, &rsa,NULL, NULL);
    }
    else
    {
        rsa = PEM_read_bio_RSAPrivateKey(keybio, &rsa,NULL, NULL);
    }
    if(rsa == NULL)
    {
        printf( "Failed to create RSA" );
    }

    return rsa;
}
```
Remember, this is just a skeleton code. You need to complete it and extend it as per your requirements like error handling, key management services integration, different modes of encryption etc. You'll also need to use other libraries for AES encryption which are available on the internet and OS level cryptographic library OpenSSL can be used. The implementation would involve understanding of cryptography algorithms, their usage in C++ programming and also how OpenSSL works with these algorithms. Moreover, you should be familiar with low-level operations like memory management, threading, logging etc.
