['Below is a simplified example of how you could implement such system in Java. Note that it's a complex task involving multiple layers including HTTP requests management, caching strategies, error handling, logging mechanism, and unit/integration tests. Here we will use Guice for dependency injection, Ehcache as the caching solution, Apache HttpClient to handle http request, Log4j for logging, GSON library to serialize and deserialize JSON data, and JUnit for testing. 

```java
import com.google.inject.*;
import org.apache.http.HttpResponse;
import org.apache.http.client.methods.CloseableHttpResponse;
import org.apache.http.client.methods.HttpGet;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.CloseableHttpClient;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.HttpClients;
import net.sf.ehcache.*;
import org.slf4j.*;
import com.google.gson.*;

public class PokemonService {
  private final CloseableHttpClient httpClient = HttpClients.createDefault();
  private final CacheManager cacheManager = CacheManager.getInstance();
  private final Cache memoryOnlyCache = new Cache("pokemon-cache",1000,false,true,7200,86400);
  private static Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(PokemonService.class);

  public String getPokemonInfo(String pokemonName) throws Exception {
    Element cacheElement = memoryOnlyCache.get(pokemonName);

    if (cacheElement != null){
      return cacheElement.getObjectValue().toString();
    } else {
      HttpGet request = new HttpGet("https://api/pokemon/" + pokemonName);
      CloseableHttpResponse response = httpClient.execute(request);

      if (response.getStatusLine().getStatusCode() == 200) {
        String jsonString = EntityUtils.toString(response.getEntity());

        memoryOnlyCache.put(new Element(pokemonName, JsonParser.parseString(jsonString)));
        return jsonString;
      } else {
        logger.error("Failed to fetch Pokemon info: " + response.getStatusLine().getReasonPhrase());
        throw new Exception("Error fetching data from external API");
      }
    }
  }
}
```
This code does not include all of your requirements, but I hope it's enough to start understanding how you might build such a system. In production-level applications, you would likely want more complex error handling and logging mechanisms, as well as more thorough testing and documentation.
']

