['This is a high-level task that requires in-depth knowledge of Rust programming language, LLVM IR and systems programming concepts like parallel processing, error handling, resource management, etc. Here's a very basic implementation layout for each module you need. Please note that this needs further expansion and implementation to fully meet the requirements.

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::thread;
use std::path::Path;
use std_semaphore::*;
use clap::Parser;
use anyhow::Result;
use serde_json::Value;

#[derive(Parser, Debug)]
struct Args {
    #[clap(short, long, default_value = "info")]
    verbosity: String,
}

fn read_config_files<P: AsRef<Path>>(path: P) -> Result<Vec<Value>> {
    let path = path.as_ref();
    let files = fs::read_dir(path)?;
    files
        .filter_map(Result::ok)
        .filter(|entry| {
            let metadata = entry.metadata().unwrap();
            metadata.is_file() && match entry.path().extension() {
                None => false,
                Some(os_str) => os_str == "json",
            }
        })
        .map(|entry| {
            let file = fs::File::open(entry.path())?;
            serde_json::from_reader(file)
        })
        .collect()
}

fn parse_configs(configs: Vec<Value>, semaphore: &CountingSemaphore, verbosity: &str) {
    let thread = thread::spawn(move || {
        for config in configs {
            // Parse the configurations and generate LLVM IR code tailored for specific hardware targets.
            println now !{verbosity}!"Thread id running: {std::thread::current().unwrap().id()}"
        }
    });
    thread.join().expect("Unable to join on thread");
    semaphore.release();
}

fn main() -> Result<()> {
    let args = Args::parse();
    let configs = read_config_files("path/to/your/configs")?;

    let semaphore = CountingSemaphore::new(0);
    for chunk in &configs.chunks(5) {
        parse_configs(chunk.to_vec(), &semaphore, &args.verbosity);
    }

    // Wait until all threads have finished executing.
    semaphore.acquire_many(10).unwrap();

    Ok(())
}
```

Please note that the above code does not include all your requirements like error handling, system integration with external version control systems or cross-compilation tools and testing frameworks. Those parts will need to be expanded significantly and likely involve using third party crates for these functionalities.
