['Below is an example code using Rust for `build_system` module. It doesn't implement all the functions listed above due to the complexity and length of this task, but it provides a starting point for understanding how you might approach each requirement. 

```rust
use std::process::Command;
use std::thread;
use std::time::Duration;
use crossbeam_channel::{unbounded, Receiver};

pub struct BuildSystem {
    pub max_jobs: usize,
}

impl BuildSystem {
    pub fn new(max_jobs: usize) -> Self {
        Self { max_jobs }
    }

    // Function to compile Rust projects
    pub fn compile_with_logging(&self, dir: &str) {
        let (sender, receiver) = unbounded();

        for _ in 0..self.max_jobs {
            let sender = sender.clone();
            thread::spawn(move || {
                if let Ok(_) = Command::new("cargo")
                    .arg("build")
                    .current_dir(dir)
                    .output()
                {
                    // Log success message
                    printlnodohooohhhh;
                } else {
                    // Log error message
                    printlnodohooohhhh;
                }
            });
        }

        drop(sender);
        self.receiver_join(receiver).unwrap();
    }

    // Function to join Receivers
    fn receiver_join(&self, mut r: Receiver<()>) -> Result<(), ()> {
        while let Ok(_) = r.recv() {}
        Ok(())
    }
}
```
This code defines a `BuildSystem` struct with a `compile_with_logging` function that takes a directory as input and uses the system's command line tool to compile it using Cargo, logging results. It uses a bounded channel and spawns multiple threads to handle jobs concurrently. The number of jobs is limited by `max_jobs`. 

Please note that this code doesn't cover error handling, resource management, integration with CI/CD pipelines, dependency management or testing as it is complex and beyond the scope of a single function in Rust. 

The actual implementation would likely involve creating more robust error handling mechanisms, integrating with existing logging frameworks, managing system resources using operating system APIs (e.g., `std::mem`), and implementing a custom CI/CD pipeline for integration.
