['This is quite an involved task that requires significant time to implement correctly. Here's a simplified example demonstrating how you can create a Rust Tokio worker which reads messages from Kafka and writes them into PostgreSQL database using async operations. Note that this code does not fully cover your operational constraints and real-world challenges, but it could be used as the starting point for creating a more robust distributed task processing system:

```rust
use tokio::sync::mpsc;
use tokio_postgres::{NoTlsConnector, Error, Row};
use kafka::consumer::StreamConsumer;
use kafka::protocol::record::Record;
use std::collections::HashMap;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let consumer = StreamConsumer::new(vec![kafka::TopicPartition {
        topic: "test".to_string(),
        partition: 0,
    }]);
    consumer.set_timeout(std::time::Duration::from_millis(100));
    let mut receiver = mpsc::channel(256);
    tokio::spawn(async move {
        loop {
            if let Err(e) = consumer.poll() {
                println_error(&format_err, e);
                return;
            }
            for record in consumer.messages() {
                receiver.send(record).await.unwrap();
            }
        }
    });
    let mut db = NoTlsConnector::new("localhost", 5432, "username", Some("password"), "database_name")?.connect().await?;
    while let Ok(record) = receiver.recv().await {
        println_message(&format_msg, record);
        db.execute("INSERT INTO test (key, value) VALUES ($1, $2)", &[&record.key, &record.value]).await?;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```
This code reads messages from Kafka, prints them out and stores in PostgreSQL database. Please note that you need to replace the connection details (like hostname, username, password etc.) according to your own setup. This is a very basic example, you would have to expand this into a full-fledged system with proper error handling, configuration parameters, testing etc.
']

['Here's a simplified example of how you could design and implement this function using Rust, focusing on creating a new subdirectory for each user under the base directory path and setting an environment variable:

```rust
use std::env;
use std::fs::{self, File};
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::sync::OnceLock;
use std::thread;
use std::time::Duration;

static USER_DATA_DIR: OnceLock<String> = OnceLock::new();

fn create_user_directory(user_id: &str, base_dir: Option<&PathBuf>) -> Result<(), String> {
    let dir = match base_dir {
        Some(base) => {
            if !fs::metadata(&**base).map_or(false, |m| m.is_dir()),
              || false {
                return Err("Invalid base directory".to_string());
            }
            base.join(user_id)
        },
        None => PathBuf::from("~/data").join(user_id),
    };

    if !fs::metadata(&dir).map_or(false, |m| m.is_dir()) {
        fs::create_dir_all(&dir)?;
    }

    USER_DATA_DIR.call_once(|| dir.into_os_string().into_string().unwrap());
    env::set_var("USER_DATA_DIR", &**USER_DATA_DIR.get().unwrap())?;

    Ok(())
}
```
