["Here is a high-level structure of how you can approach this problem. For brevity, I'm only showing the main functions, you need to extend it for your specific needs. 

Note: This is a simplified example, and actual production code will be more complex, with additional considerations like error tracking, caching, different dependency handling methods etc.

```javascript
class ModuleLoader {
    constructor(basePath) {
        this.loadedModules = {};
        this.modulesToLoad = [];
        this.dependencies = {};
        this.basePath = basePath;
    }

    loadModule(moduleName, dependencies, callback) {
        // If the module has already been loaded, return it immediately
        if (this.loadedModules[moduleName]) {
            callback(null, this.loadedModules[moduleName]);
            return;
        }

        const script = document.createElement('script');
        script.src = `${this.basePath}/${moduleName}.js`;
        script.onload = () => {
            // Mark the module as loaded and trigger its callbacks
            this.loadedModules[moduleName] = window[moduleName];
            for (let i = 0; i < this.modulesToLoad.length; i++) {
                if (this.modulesToLoad[i].name === moduleName) {
                    this.modulesToLoad.splice(i, 1);
                    this.modulesToLoad[i].callback(null, window[moduleName]);
                    break;
                }
            }
        };

        script.onerror = (err) => {
            // Call the callbacks with an error if the module fails to load
            for (let i = 0; i < this.modulesToLoad.length; i++) {
                if (this.modulesToLoad[i].name === moduleName) {
                    this.modulesToLoad.splice(i, 1);
                    this.modulesToLoad[i].callback(err);
                    break;
                }
            }
        };

        document.body.appendChild(script);
    }

    require(moduleNames, callback) {
        const unloadedModules = moduleNames.filter((name) => !this.loadedModules[name]);

        if (unloadedModules.length === 0) {
            // All modules are loaded, return them immediately
            callback(null, moduleNames.map((name) => this.loadedModules[name]));
            return;
        }

        // Queue the callback until all required modules have been loaded
        this.modulesToLoad.push({ names: unloadedModules, callback });
    }
}
```

For handling asynchronous module loading and execution, we use promises or async/await constructs which would make your code more readable and manageable in case of complex dependency graphs. Also, this loader doesn't handle external data source integrations due to their complexity and specific nature.

This is just a very basic example. A production-level module system will include caching modules for faster load times, handling circular references, versioning conflicts, error tracking/logging, unit testing etc., in addition to the points you mentioned. Also, this code doesn't account for potential performance bottlenecks such as network latency or high concurrency due to these factors affecting multiple parts of a system, not just the module loader itself.
"]

