Here is a basic example of an SSL client with resource management and error handling:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.nio.channels.*;
import javax.net.ssl.*;

public class SSLClient {
    private static final int MAX_BUFFER = 1024;

    public void startClient() throws Exception {
        // Create a file instance for the client certificate
        FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("client.pfx");

        // Load the key store
        KeyStore ks = KeyStore.getInstance("pkcs12");
        ks.load(fis, "password".toCharArray());

        // Initialize the SSLContext for TLS/SSL connections
        SSLContext sslContext = SSLContext.getDefault();

        // Create a key manager
        KeyManagerFactory kmf = KeyManagerFactory.getInstance("SunX509");
        kmf.init(ks, "password".toCharArray());

        // Create a trust manager
        TrustManagerFactory tmf = TrustManagerFactory.getInstance("SunX509");
        tmf.init(ks);

        sslContext.init(kmf.getKeyManagers(), tmf.getTrustManagers(), null);

        // Create a socket channel and connect to the server
        SocketChannel client = SocketChannel.open();
        client.configureBlocking(false);
        client.connect(new InetSocketAddress("localhost", 8001));

        while(!client.finishConnect()) {
            // wait for connection to be established
        }

        SSLSocket sslSocket = (SSLSocket) sslContext.getSocketFactory().createSocket(client.socket(), "localhost", 8001, false);

        try {
            // Perform the SSL handshake
            sslSocket.startHandshake();

            ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(MAX_BUFFER);

            while (client.read(buffer) != -1) {
                buffer.flip();

                // process data from server

                buffer.clear();
            }
        } catch (SSLException ex) {
            // Handle SSL exception
            ex.printStackTrace();
        } finally {
            sslSocket.close();
        }
    }
}
```

This is a very basic example and doesn't include many of the features you mentioned, like connection pooling, external dependency management, load balancing, failover strategies, redundancy handling, configuration management, maintainability, and testing. Each of these would require additional code to handle their respective functionalities. 

