```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class InitializationService {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<Class<? extends Initializable>, Future<?>> initializers = 
        new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void initAndValidate(Initializable... components) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
        for (Initializable component : components) {
            if (!initializers.containsKey(component.getClass())) {
                FutureTask<?> future = new FutureTask<>(() -> 
                    component.initialize());
                initializers.putIfAbsent(component.getClass(), future);
                executorService.submit(future);
            } 
        }

        for (Initializable component : components) {
            if (!component.validate()) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("Validation failed: " + component.getClass().getName());
            }
        }
    }

    // Other methods and classes
}
```

In the above code, each initialization is performed in a separate thread using `FutureTask` which provides better control over the execution of tasks than just calling `run()` method directly. This also allows us to monitor their statuses concurrently without blocking. 

