["Creating a complete C++ library as described is beyond the scope of this platform, as it involves multiple modules, extensive error handling, integration with various systems, security measures, optimization strategies, and test suites. However, I can provide you with a simple example illustrating how to create a thread-safe queue in C++:

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

template <typename T>
class SafeQueue {
    std::queue<T> q;
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::condition_variable cv;
public:
    void enqueue(T t) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        q.push(t);
        cv.notify_one();
    }

    T dequeue() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        while (q.empty()) {
            cv.wait(lock);
        }
        T val = q.front();
        q.pop();
        return val;
    }
};
```

This class provides a thread-safe queue with the enqueue and dequeue operations, where `dequeue()` blocks if there's nothing to return (an empty queue). This example is quite rudimentary but should be enough for illustrating how to create a thread-safe data structure in C++. 

However, creating such a complete library would require an extensive understanding of multithreading and network programming, as well as careful design considerations regarding scalability, performance tuning, security measures, etc. It's advisable to approach this kind of project from the ground up, starting with small components and gradually building up the functionality you need. In addition to C++ knowledge, it would be necessary to have knowledge about libraries like Boost Asio for network programming, RESTful services using frameworks like Restbed or Poco, JSON parsing using libraries like jsoncpp or nlohmann/json, etc.

