```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ThreadPoolExample {
    private ExecutorService executor;

    public void startThreadPool() {
        int corePoolSize = 5;  // minimum number of threads
        int maxPoolSize = 10;   // maximum number of threads
        long keepAliveTime = 60L;  // time idle threads will wait before terminating

        executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(corePoolSize, maxPoolSize, keepAliveTime, TimeUnit.SECONDS, new LinkedBlockingQueue<Runnable>());
    }

    public void executeTask(Runnable task) {
        executor.execute(task);
    }
}
```
This example starts a thread pool with 5 threads and allows up to 10 additional threads to be created, each idle for up to 60 seconds before being terminated. You can then execute tasks on this thread pool using the `executor.execute` method.

Here's an example of how you could set up a file watcher:

```java
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class FileWatcher {
    private WatchService watcher;

    public void startWatching(Path path) throws IOException {
        watcher = FileSystems.getDefault().newWatchService();

        // register the directory with the watch service
        path.register(watcher, StandardWatchEventKinds.ENTRY_MODIFY);

        while (true) {
            WatchKey key;
            try {
                key = watcher.poll(25, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);  // wait for a file change
            } catch (InterruptedException ex) {
                return;
            }

            if (key == null) {
                continue;  // loop until a key is available
            }

            // process all events in this key
            for (WatchEvent<?> event : key.pollEvents()) {
                System.out.println("File changed: " + event.context());
            }

            boolean valid = key.reset();  // reset the key for further polling
            if (!valid) {
                break;  // exit loop if the key is not valid (e.g., directory was deleted)
            }
        }

        watcher.close();  // close the watch service
    }
}
```
This example starts watching a directory for changes and prints a message whenever a file in that directory is modified. Please note, you need to adapt this code to your needs and implement error handling etc. Also, please note that the use of `FileSystems`, `Path`, `WatchService`, `StandardWatchEventKinds` and `WatchEvent` requires Java 7 or later.
']

