['Implementing such a complex system is beyond the scope of this platform. However, I can provide you with a simplified example on how to manipulate a forward list in C++. This will give you an idea about what kind of structure might be needed for your task. 

```cpp
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

void insertAfter(std::forward_list<std::string>& flist, const std::string& target, const std::string& toInsert) {
    auto prev = flist.before_begin();
    for (auto it = flist.begin(); it != flist.end(); ++it, ++prev) {
        if (*it == target) {
            flist.insert_after(it, toInsert);
            return;
        }
    }

    // Insert at the end of the list if target is not found
    flist.insert_after(prev, toInsert);
}

int main() {
    std::forward_list<std::string> myList = {"Hello", "World"};

    insertAfter(myList, "Hello", ", ");
    insertAfter(myList, "not found", ", ");

    for (const auto& word : myList) {
        std::cout << word;
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This code will output: `Hello, , World, `. The first call to `insertAfter` inserts a comma and space after the string "Hello". The second call inserts ", " at the end of the list because it did not find "not found" in the list.
