['This is quite an extensive task to be handled by one code block as it involves multiple complex features such as thread management, resource handling, data parsing/validating, error handling mechanisms, service integration, OAuth-based authentication, rate limiting, and comprehensive monitoring system. 

It would be better to break this down into smaller tasks or modules that can each be developed separately, then combined into a complete application at the end. Developing such an application would require good understanding of C++ programming, operating systems concepts, networking protocols, security measures and performance analysis tools.

For example, for running command in background you can use `fork()` and `execvp()` system calls like so:

```c
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int runInBackground(char *command) {
    pid_t pid = fork();

    if (pid == -1) {
        // Fork failed
        return 0;
    } else if (pid == 0) {
        // Child process
        char* const argv[] = {"sh", "-c", command, NULL};
        execvp(argv[0], argv);

        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);  // exec only returns on failure
    } else {
        int status;
        waitpid(pid, &status, WUNTRACED | WCONTINUED);

        if (WIFEXITED(status)) {
            return WEXITSTATUS(status) == 0 ? 1 : 0;  // Return success only if child process exited with status code 0
        } else {
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
```

This function first forks the current process, creating a new one. The child process executes `command` in a shell using `execvp()`. If `fork()` fails or if `execvp()` returns, this function will return 0 indicating failure. Otherwise, it waits until the child process has exited and then checks its exit status to determine whether the command was successful (exit code 0) or not.
']

