While I can provide a simplified example that does not fully cover all your requirements, here is a basic example of how you might start a cross-platform library in C:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct {
    int value;
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
} Cell;

void* worker(void *arg) {
    Cell *cell = (Cell *)arg;

    // Lock the mutex to ensure thread-safety.
    pthread_mutex_lock(&cell->mutex);

    cell->value++;  // Increment the value in this cell.

    printf("Value is now %d\n", cell->value);

    // Unlock the mutex to allow other threads access.
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&cell->mutex);

    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    Cell *cells = malloc(sizeof(Cell) * 10);
    pthread_t *threads = malloc(sizeof(pthread_t) * 10);

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        cells[i].value = i;

        // Initialize the mutex.
        pthread_mutex_init(&cells[i].mutex, NULL);

        pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, worker, &cells[i]);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);  // Wait for all threads to finish.
    }

    free(threads);
    free(cells);
}
```

This code creates an array of 'Cell' structures, each of which contains a value and a mutex. It then creates ten worker threads that each increment the value in one cell while holding the corresponding mutex. 

Please note that this is just a simplistic example and doesn't cover many aspects you specified like error handling, dynamic configuration scaling, logging, RESTful API design, XML parsing and OAuth2 implementation, etc. You would need to extend and adapt this code to fit into your requirements in a more complex library.
