['This is a complex task which can't be fully achieved in few lines of code. Here is a simplified structure to start off with: 

```java
import java.security.*;
import javax.crypto.*;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;

public class EncryptionFramework {
    private KeyGenerator keygen;
    private Cipher cipher;
    private SecretKey secretKey;

    public EncryptionFramework(String algorithm) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException, NoSuchPaddingException{
        keygen = KeyGenerator.getInstance(algorithm);
        cipher = Cipher.getInstance(algorithm);
        secretKey = keygen.generateKey();
    }

    public byte[] encryptText(String msg, String algorithm) throws InvalidKeyException, IllegalBlockSizeException, BadPaddingException {
        byte[] text = msg.getBytes();
        cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, secretKey);
        return cipher.doFinal(text);
    }

    public String decryptText(byte[] msg, String algorithm) throws InvalidKeyException, IllegalBlockSizeException, BadPaddingException {
        cipher.init(Cipher.DECRYPT_MODE, secretKey);
        return new String(cipher.doFinal(msg));
    } 

    public static void main(String[] args) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException, NoSuchPaddingException, InvalidKeyException, IllegalBlockSizeException, BadPaddingException {
        EncryptionFramework encryptor = new EncryptionFramework("AES");
        byte[] encryptedMsg = encryptor.encryptText("Hello World", "AES");
        System.out.println(new String(encryptedMsg));
        System.out.println(encryptor.decryptText(encryptedMsg, "AES")); 
    }
}
```
This is a simple AES encryption framework that doesn't include the full complexity you've asked for and requires a lot more work to implement fully including secure key management system with KMS, NIO channels etc. You may need to look into libraries like Bouncy Castle or Java Cryptography Architecture (JCA) to handle these complexities efficiently. Also, remember that dealing with cryptography should always be done by security professionals when it comes to high-security applications, as incorrect usage of cryptographic primitives can lead to serious vulnerabilities in the software.
