typedef struct node {
    NICB *nicb;
    struct node *next;
} Node;

Node *head = NULL; // head of the list

// Function declarations
void add_NICB(char *mac);
void remove_NICB(char *mac);
void update_IPs();
```
Here, a simple linked list structure is used to store NICBs. The lock variable ensures thread safety and prevents concurrent accesses from multiple threads. Note that the MAC address is just an example field; you might want to use a more complex data type depending on your actual needs.

The `add_NICB` function could look something like this:
```c
void add_NICB(char *mac) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // acquire the lock before modifying the list

    Node *newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    newNode->nicb = malloc(sizeof(NICB));
    newNode->nicb->mac = mac;

    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // release the lock after modifying the list
}
```

The `remove_NICB` function could look something like this:
```c
void remove_NICB(char *mac) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // acquire the lock before modifying the list

    Node **tracer = &head;
    while (*tracer != NULL) {
        if (strcmp((*tracer)->nicb->mac, mac) == 0) {
            Node *temp = *tracer;
            *tracer = temp->next;
            free(temp->nicb); // free memory allocated for NICB
            free(temp);       // free node itself
            break;
        }
        tracer = &(*tracer)->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // release the lock after modifying the list
}
```
The `update_IPs` function can be based on periodic callbacks:
```c
void update_IPs() {
    while (1) {
        sleep(interval);  // sleep for some interval before updating IPs

        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // acquire the lock to access and modify NICBs

        Node *current = head;
        while (current != NULL) {
            // update IP configurations here, consider using condition variables 
            // or other synchronization mechanisms for concurrency control.

            current = current->next;
        }

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // release the lock after updating IPs
    }
}
```
You would start a new thread to call `update_IPs` periodically using pthread library in main function. 

The above is just an example, and doesn't handle some aspects like DNS lookups or network configuration services. For such operations, you might want to use system-level interfaces provided by your operating system (like POSIX API or Windows APIs) for network administration tasks. Error handling can be done using `errno` variable in case of any error and logs can be maintained separately.
