['Creating such an extensive library involves several aspects that are beyond the capacity of this text-based interface to fully document. However, I can provide a simple example on how you could start structuring your codebase using C. The below code is a very basic representation of how you might begin to implement network interface control blocks in a thread-safe way.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct NICB {
    char mac_address[20];
    char ip_configuration[16]; // assuming IPv4 address
    int port;
} NICB;

NICB nics[100]; // array of 100 NICBs for example purposes
int count = 0; // number of currently stored NICBs
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void *addNic(void *arg) {
    char mac[20];
    char ip[16];
    int port;

    // Generate MAC, IP, and port for this NICB
    sprintf(mac, "MAC_%d", count);
    sprintf(ip, "IP_%d", count);
    port = rand() % 10 + 1024;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // lock before accessing shared data

    strcpy(nics[count].mac_address, mac);
    strcpy(nics[count].ip_configuration, ip);
    nics[count].port = port;
    count++;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // unlock after modifying shared data

    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    srand(time(NULL)); // seed the random number generator

    pthread_t threads[10]; // create 10 threads for example purposes

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        if (pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, addNic, NULL)) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Error creating thread\n");
            return 1;
        }
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) { // join all threads
        if (pthread_join(threads[i], NULL)) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Error joining thread\n");
            return 2;
        }
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) { // print all NICBs created by threads
        printf("MAC: %s IP: %s Port: %d\n", nics[i].mac_address, nics[i].ip_configuration, nics[i].port);
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In the above example, a struct is used to store NICB (network interface control block) data and an array of these structures is used to represent all possible network interfaces. The 'addNic' function is run in multiple threads, each creating its own unique NICB with a random MAC address, IP configuration, and port number before storing the NICB into the shared 'nics' array.

Please note that this example is very basic and does not fully represent all aspects of your requirement, but it should give you a starting point on how to manage multiple network interfaces in C with thread-safety. You would need to add more code to implement the rest of your requirements, such as integrating with external services, handling dynamic updates, health monitoring etc.
']

["Here's an outline of how you might implement some parts of this service in Node.js:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const cacheManager = require('cache-manager');
const axios = require('axios');
const circuitBreaker = require('opossum');
const PromisePool = require('@supercharge/promise-pool');

// Create an Express.js app
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

// Create a cache manager instance using LRU (Least Recently Used) strategy for eviction policy
const memoryCache = cacheManager.caching({ 
    store: 'memory',
    ttl: 60 * 60, // Time to Live in seconds
    max: 10000, // Maximum number of items in the cache
    algorithm: 'lru'
});

// Create a circuit breaker instance for error recovery strategy
const breaker = new circuitBreaker(axios.get, {
    timeout: 3000,
    errorThresholdPercentage: 50, // If more than half of requests fail, the breaker will open
});
breaker.fallback(() => 'API is down');

// API endpoint
app.get('/stock-data', async (req, res) => {
    const cacheKey = req.url;

    // Check if data exists in cache
    const cachedData = await memoryCache.wrap(cacheKey, () => breaker.fire());

    // Send cached data or fetch new data if cache is not hit
    res.send(cachedData);
});

// Async processing using PromisePool for concurrency handling and load balancing
const promiseProducer = function*(iterable) {
    let i = 0;

    while (i < iterable.length) {
      yield new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        process.nextTick(
          () => iterable[i] % 2 ? resolve(iterable[i++]) : reject(new Error('error'))
        );
      });
    }
};

const pool = new PromisePool(() => promiseProducer(['url1', 'url2']), 5)
    .forEach((result) => {
        console.log(result);
    })
    .catch((error, results, rejections) => {
        console.error('Promise Pool Error:', error);
        console.error('Successful Promises:', results);
        console.error('Failed Promises:', rejections);
    });

// Automated tests
const assert = require('assert');
it('should return cached data when available', async function() {
    // Mock cache hit
});
```
This is a simplified example that doesn't cover all the requirements you mentioned (e.g., error recovery, concurrency handling), but it shows how to use Express.js for routing and creating an API endpoint, `cache-manager` for caching layer, `opossum` for circuit breaker, `@supercharge/promise-pool` for async processing, and `assert` for automated tests.
