As a result, it would be far more productive to implement this kind of system in a higher-level language with better support for concurrency, networking, distributed systems, REST APIs and databases like Python or Java (using Spring Boot). However, I can provide you an example of how such a library might start from a very basic point:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

typedef struct NetworkConfiguration {
    int frequency_band;
    int bandwidth;
    int cell_identity;
} NetworkConfig;

typedef struct Pool {
    NetworkConfig **configs;
    size_t count;
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
    sem_t semaphore;
} Pool;

void pool_init(Pool *pool, int initial_size) {
    pool->count = initial_size;
    pool->configs = (NetworkConfig **) malloc(initial_size * sizeof(NetworkConfig*));
    pthread_mutex_init(&pool->mutex, NULL);
    sem_init(&pool->semaphore, 0, initial_size);
}

void pool_add(Pool *pool, NetworkConfig *config) {
    sem_wait(&pool->semaphore); // Decrease the semaphore count by 1. Wait if it's already zero.

    pthread_mutex_lock(&pool->mutex);
    pool->count++;
    pool->configs = (NetworkConfig **) realloc(pool->configs, pool->count * sizeof(NetworkConfig*)); // Resize the array if necessary.
    pool->configs[pool->count - 1] = config;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&pool->mutex);
}

void pool_remove(Pool *pool, int index) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&pool->mutex);

    // Remove the element by replacing it with the last one and decreasing count.
    free(pool->configs[index]); 
    pool->count--;
    if (index < pool->count) {
        pool->configs[index] = pool->configs[pool->count];
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&pool->mutex);

    sem_post(&pool->semaphore); // Increase the semaphore count by 1.
}
```
Please note, this is a very basic example and does not cover all requirements mentioned in your question. It's also worth noting that handling threads manually in C can be error-prone because of the difficulty to debug these issues. This is why usually high level languages with built-in support for these features are used instead.
"]

