['This task requires several steps to design a highly available, scalable distributed caching solution for an e-commerce platform with Java as programming language. This involves understanding of many complex concepts such as distributed systems, data replication, event-driven architecture, and performance optimization. 

Below is an example of how this could be implemented in Java using popular frameworks like Spring Boot for the application framework, Hazelcast IMDG for the caching solution, Resilience4j for circuit breaker and retry mechanism, etc. Due to complexity of actual code generation for such system, below is just a simple example. For actual implementation, this needs to be developed in more depth considering all constraints and dependencies.

```java
import com.hazelcast.config.*;
import com.hazelcast.core.*;
import io.github.resilience4j.retry.Retry;
import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.CircuitBreaker;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

@Service
public class ProductCache {
    private final IMap<String, Product> productCache;  // Cache Map
    private final Supplier<Product> getFromExternalAPI;

    @Autowired
    public ProductCache(HazelcastInstance hazelcastInstance, InventoryAPIClient inventoryAPIClient) {
        this.productCache = hazelcastInstance.getMap("products");

        // Retry mechanism with exponential backoff
        Retry retryConfig = Retry.ofDefaults("externalAPI-retry");
        CircuitBreaker circuitBreaker = CircuitBreaker.ofDefaults("externalAPI-cb");

        this.getFromExternalAPI = () -> {
            try {
                return Retry.decorateSupplier(retryConfig, inventoryAPIClient::fetchProduct).apply();
            } catch (Exception e) {
                if (!circuitBreaker.isCallPermitted()) {
                    throw new RuntimeException("External API is unavailable");
                } else {
                    circuitBreaker.onError(0, e);
                    throw e;
                }
            }
        };
    }

    public Product getProduct(String productId) {
        return productCache.computeIfAbsent(productId, key -> getFromExternalAPI.get());
    }
}
```

The actual implementation would require many more components and additional configuration for error handling, performance optimization, event-driven architecture integration, etc. It is important to note that the solution would need a comprehensive testing and monitoring infrastructure in place, including logs and metrics tracking system, which are not covered by this example code. Also, it's highly recommended to use an existing caching framework/library like Hazelcast IMDG, or Spring Cache Abstraction for easier management of cache eviction policies, failover mechanisms, etc. 

Please note that designing such a system requires significant knowledge about distributed systems and advanced concepts in Java programming. It's recommended to involve senior developers or experienced architects who can handle the task effectively.
