["Implementing such a library would require extensive experience and understanding of C++ programming, concurrency, networking, system design principles, etc. Here's a simple example of how you might create a base class for managing TCP connections:

```cpp
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <mutex>
#include <memory>
#include <thread>
#include <iostream>

class TcpConnectionManager {
public:
    using Connection = std::shared_ptr<boost::asio::ip::tcp::socket>;

    explicit TcpConnectionManager(const std::string& address, unsigned short port) : 
        io_service(), socket_(io_service), endpoint_(boost::asio::ip::address::from_string(address), port) {}

    void start() {
        doConnect();
        io_service.run();
    }

private:
    boost::asio::io_service io_service;
    boost::asio::ip::tcp::socket socket_;
    boost::asio::ip::tcp::endpoint endpoint_;
    std::mutex mutex_;

    void doConnect() {
        socket_.async_connect(endpoint_, [this](boost::system::error_code ec) {
            if(!ec) {
                // Connection successful.
            } else {
                // Connection failed, handle error.
            }

            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
            if(!io_service.stopped()) doConnect();
        });
    }
};
```

In a real-world scenario, you would likely have more complex classes to manage connections and interactions with other services in a distributed environment. You might use libraries such as Boost.Asio or Poco C++ Libraries to handle networking and concurrency. The library design might involve modular components for handling different aspects of TCP connection management (e.g., connection pooling, load balancing, error recovery), and would need extensive testing and performance profiling to ensure it meets the requirements outlined in your task.
