['Below is a high-level overview of how you could implement such an agent using Java. For brevity reasons, some details are omitted for clarity. 

```java
public class MonitoringAgent {
    private RESTAPIService restService;
    private MessagingBroker messagingBroker;
    private CloudLoggingService loggingService;
    private ExecutorService executorService;

    // Configure all these services with proper retry and backoff logic, circuit breakers etc. 
    public MonitoringAgent() {
        this.restService = new RESTAPIService();
        this.messagingBroker = new MessagingBroker();
        this.loggingService = new CloudLoggingService();
        this.executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10); // For async processing and concurrency control 
    }

    public void startMonitoring() {
        while (true) {
            Future<Void> restCheckFuture = executorService.submit(() -> checkRESTAPI());
            Future<Void> messagingBrokerCheckFuture = executorService.submit(() -> checkMessagingBroker());

            try {
                // Wait for both checks to complete
                restCheckFuture.get();
                messagingBrokerCheckFuture.get();

                Thread.sleep(5000);  // sleep for 5 seconds between each cycle
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // handle exceptions appropriately
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }

    private void checkRESTAPI() {
        try {
            String response = restService.getHealthStatus();
            loggingService.log(response);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // handle exceptions and implement retry logic here
        }
    }

    private void checkMessagingBroker() {
        try {
            String message = messagingBroker.getNextMessage();
            loggingService.log(message);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // handle exceptions and implement retry logic here
        }
    }
}
```

In the above code, `RESTAPIService`, `MessagingBroker`, and `CloudLoggingService` are placeholders for actual classes that interact with these services. 

