```csharp
using System;
using System.Text;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class PaymentGatewayHandler : IPaymentGatewayHandler
{
    private readonly ILogger<PaymentGatewayHandler> _logger;
    public PaymentGatewayHandler(ILogger<PaymentGatewayHandler> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    // Asynchronous method to process transactions
    public async Task ProcessTransactionAsync(string transactionData, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        try 
        {
            // Validate input
            if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(transactionData))
                throw new ArgumentException("Invalid transaction data");

            // Use MD5HashAsync function to hash transaction data
            var hashedTransaction = await MD5HashAsync(transactionData, cancellationToken);

            _logger.LogInformation($"Hashed transaction: {hashedTransaction}");
        } 
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // Handle exceptions and log errors
            _logger.LogError(ex, "An error occurred while processing transaction");
            throw;
        }
    }

    private async Task<string> MD5HashAsync(string input, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default) 
    {
        // Check if string is null or empty before hashing
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(input))
            return string.Empty;

        using var md5 = MD5.Create();
        byte[] inputBytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(input);
        byte[] hashBytes = await md5.ComputeHashAsync(new MemoryStream(inputBytes), cancellationToken);

        return Convert.ToHexString(hashBytes);
    }
}
```
This is a simple example of how to implement the `MD5` hashing function in the given task. In real world application, you will need more complex error handling and logging mechanisms as well as proper validation for inputs. 

Regarding design choices:
- I've used an async/await pattern which is a good choice for IO-bound operations like network calls or database queries. This allows the thread to be released back to the thread pool, preventing blocking of threads and improving system throughput capacity.
- For logging, I used Microsoft's ILogger interface. This is a common practice in .NET core applications as it provides an abstraction over different types of logging mechanisms (like Serilog or NLog) and supports structured logging which can be useful for debugging and auditing purposes.
- Error handling is centralized using try/catch blocks inside the `ProcessTransactionAsync` method, so that all errors are logged with a single line of code. This makes it easier to maintain and extend in the future. 
- The use of cancellation token allows long running operations to be cancelled if necessary.

To implement more complex features like concurrent transaction processing, load balancing, error handling, security measures etc., you would need to leverage .NET Core features like gRPC for communication, Polly for resiliency patterns, etc. Also, this code needs to interact with multiple payment gateways and handle real-time analytics, which are complex tasks in themselves requiring a proper design and implementation strategy.
