['Here is an abstract view of a library design that would fulfill your requirements. This does not cover all details due to complexity, but it should give you a good idea of the overall structure.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <functional>
#include <vector>
#include <future>
#include <utility>
#include <unordered_map>
// Forward declaration of external dependencies
class TimeSeriesDatabase;
class StreamingPlatform;
class AnalyticsService;
class ExternalConfigService;
class SensorDataProcessor {
public:
    // Constructor, initializes the worker threads.
    SensorDataProcessor(size_t numWorkers) : stop{false} {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < numWorkers; ++i) {
            workers.emplace_back([this] { this->workerThread(); });
        }
    }
    ~SensorDataProcessor() {
        // Stop all threads.
        stop = true;
        cv.notify_all();
        for (auto& worker : workers) {
            if (worker.joinable()) {
                worker.join();
            }
        }
    }

    std::future<void> submitTask(std::function<void()> task) {
        // Submit a task to the queue and return a future.
        auto packagedTask = std::packaged_task<void()>(task);
        auto future = packagedTask.get_future();
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock{mtx};
            tasks.emplace(std::move(packagedTask));
        }
        cv.notify_one();
        return future;
    }

private:
    // The worker thread function.
    void workerThread() {
        while (true) {
            std::function<void()> task;

            {
                std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock{mtx};
                cv.wait(lock, [this] { return stop || !tasks.empty(); });

                if (stop && tasks.empty()) {
                    break;
                }

                task = std::move(tasks.front());
                tasks.pop();
            }

            // Execute the task.
            try {
                task();
            } catch (const std::exception& e) {
                // Handle exceptions.
            }
        }
    }

    bool stop;
    mutable std::mutex mtx;
    std::condition_variable cv;
    std::queue<std::packaged_task<void()>> tasks;
    std::vector<std::thread> workers;
};
```
The `SensorDataProcessor` class is responsible for handling all tasks. It uses a thread pool model, where the main task queue dispatches tasks to worker threads that perform them. All I/O operations are handled by separate threads to prevent blocking of the main event loop, which makes it suitable for real-time applications like sensor data processing.
The `submitTask` function allows clients to submit a new task and receive a future result immediately. It uses a lock_guard to ensure thread safety when accessing tasks queue.

Please note that designing a complete library in C++ would be much more complex than this example, considering the various integration points with external systems mentioned and all possible performance optimizations for heavy load conditions. This should provide an understanding of how you might organize your code into classes and methods to implement these features. A detailed implementation may require significant additional design and development effort.
']

