```rust
fn process_line(line: &str) -> Option<f64> {
    let mut parts = line.split(' ');
    let first_num = match parts.next() {
        Some(part) => part.parse::<f64>().ok(),
        None => return None,
    };
    let second_num = match parts.next_back() {
        Some(part) => part.parse::<f64>().ok(),
        None => return None,
    };
    first_num.zip(second_num).map(|(a, b)| (a + b) / 2.0)
}
```

3. **Implementing retry logic** with exponential backoff:
```rust
async fn perform_operation() -> Result<()> {
    let mut attempt = 1;
    loop {
        match operation().await {
            Ok(_) => break,
            Err(e) if attempt < 5 => {
                println::"Operation failed. Retrying...");
                tokio::time::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_secs(2u64.pow(attempt))).await;
                attempt += 1;
            }
            Err(e) => return Err(e),
        }
    }
}
```

Please refer to the official Rust, Tokio, PostgreSQL, RabbitMQ, Cassandra, and Kubernetes documentation for further guidance on how to implement these parts of your solution. And remember that writing unit tests, integration tests, and configuring logging/monitoring solutions will also form a significant part of this task.
