However, I can provide a simplified example for creating an IoT configuration management system in Rust that integrates with third-party data sources and supports version control. Please note that this is not an exhaustive solution but rather a starting point:

```rust
extern crate serde; // 1.0.98
#[macro_use]
extern crate serde_derive; // 1.0.98
extern crate reqwest; // 0.10
extern crate toml; // 0.5.6
use std::fs;
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::collections::HashMap;

#[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]
struct Config {
    version: String,
}

pub struct ConfigurationManager {
    config_dir: PathBuf,
    data_sources: HashMap<String, reqwest::Url>,
    current_config: Option<Config>,
}

impl ConfigurationManager {
    pub fn new(config_dir: PathBuf) -> Self {
        Self {
            config_dir,
            data_sources: HashMap::new(),
            current_config: None,
        }
    }

    pub async fn fetch(&mut self) {
        let manager = self.data_sources.get("manager").unwrap();
        let res = reqwest::get(manager).await.unwrap().text().await.unwrap();
        let config: Config = toml::from_str(&res).unwrap();

        fs::write(self.config_dir.join("config.toml"), &res).unwrap();

        self.current_config = Some(config);
    }
}
```
In this example, a `ConfigurationManager` struct is created that fetches configuration data from an external API and saves it to the local filesystem. The actual implementation would be more complex with error handling, version control, etc., but this should give you a starting point. 

