["The requirements are extensive and complex to be fully implemented within this text format or in a single code block. Here's a simplified start to illustrate how you might structure your C++ program. This includes classes for representing geolocation data and caching, but the actual implementation would require significant work on integration with external services, concurrency control, distributed locking mechanisms, scalability, resource management, error handling, monitoring and logging, trade-offs, and unit tests.

```cpp
#include <unordered_map>
#include <chrono>

class GeoLocation {
public:
    double latitude;
    double longitude;
    double elevation;
    std::chrono::system_clock::time_point timestamp;
    std::string sourceMetadata;
};

class LRUCacheEntry {
public:
    std::chrono::system_clock::time_point expiryTime;
    GeoLocation geolocation;
};

class LRUCache {
private:
    int capacity;
    std::unordered_map<std::string, list<pair<int, int>>::iterator> ma;
    list<pair<int, int>> lru; // pair(key, value)
public:
    LRUCache(int);
    int get(int);
    void set(int, int);
};

LRUCache::LRUCache(int capacity) {
    this->capacity = capacity;
}

int LRUCache::get(int key) {
    if (ma.find(key) == ma.end()) return -1; // Not found in cache

    // Move the accessed item to the front of list as it's recently used
    lru.splice(lru.begin(), lru, ma[key]); 

    return ma[key]->second; // Return value from cache
}

void LRUCache::set(int key, int value) {
    if (ma.find(key) == ma.end()) { // Not in cache
        lru.push_front({key, value});
        capacity--;
        ma[key] = lru.begin();

        if(capacity < 0) { 
            auto lastItemIterator = --lru.end();
            ma.erase(lastItemIterator->first); // Remove from map
            lru.pop_back(); // Remove from list as it's least recently used
        }
    } else {
        lru.splice(lru.begin(), lru, ma[key]); 
        ma[key]->second = value;  // Update the value in cache
    }
}
```
Please note that this is a basic LRU Cache implementation and doesn't handle time-to-live (TTL) policy which could be added as part of `set` method. Also, this does not include distributed locking mechanisms or any performance benchmarks, error handling, logging, etc. which are critical components for your system.
