```javascript
//services/QueueService.js
const Queue = require('bull');
let jobQueue;

class QueueService {
  constructor(name) {
    this.queueName = name;
    jobQueue = new Queue(this.queueName, 'redis://127.0.0.1:6379');
  }

  addJobToQueue(jobData){
      return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
          jobQueue.add(jobData).then((job)=>{
              resolve(job);
          }).catch((err) => {
              reject(err);
          });
      })
  }
}
```

This is a basic example of how you might use the `bull` library to implement a task queue in Node.js, which supports various backends including Redis. Note that error handling should be improved for real-world usage and also note that this doesn't include any retry mechanism or thread safety as these are complex topics.

You would add a new `QueueService` instance to your `app.js` and utilize its methods when necessary, like in the following example:

```javascript
