["Your task is quite complex and beyond the scope of this platform to fully implement a full C++ library with all its features. However, I can give you a starting point on how to structure such a system and guide you through the main components. 

Here is a high-level overview of your project structure:
```cpp
// DynamicMediaProcessor.h
#pragma once

#include <queue> // for std::priority_queue
#include <thread> // for std::thread
#include <mutex> // for std::mutex, std::unique_lock
#include <condition_variable> // for std::condition_variable
#include <future> // for std::promise, std::packaged_task

class DynamicMediaProcessor {
public:
    DynamicMediaProcessor();
    ~DynamicMediaProcessor();

    void addTask(std::function<void()> task);
    std::future<void> submitTask(std::function<void()> task);

private:
    struct Task {
        uint8_t priority;
        std::packaged_task<void()> job;

        bool operator<(const Task& rhs) const { return priority < rhs.priority; }
    };

    void workerThread();

    bool shutdown = false;
    std::mutex queueMutex, waitForTasksMutex;
    std::condition_variable waitForTasks;
    std::queue<Task> tasks;
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
};
```
This is a basic implementation of a thread pool with prioritized tasks. It uses `std::packaged_task` to wrap the tasks into promises that can be returned as futures. The priority and the actual job are wrapped in a struct, and this struct can be compared using the `<` operator for sorting purposes.

Here is a starting point for the workerThread function:
```cpp
// DynamicMediaProcessor.cpp
void DynamicMediaProcessor::workerThread() {
    while (!shutdown) {
        std::packaged_task<void()> task;

        { // scope to ensure mutex is always unlocked when going out of scope
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(queueMutex);

            waitForTasks.wait(lock, [this] { return shutdown || !tasks.empty(); });

            if (shutdown) break;

            task = std::move(tasks.front().job); // move the job to local variable
            tasks.pop(); // remove it from the queue
        }

        task(); // execute the task
    }
}
```
This is a basic worker thread that continuously waits for new tasks and executes them when available. The tasks are taken out of the queue under lock, so they can't be intercepted by another thread while being executed.

Please note that this code doesn't include all the features you asked for such as:
- OAuth 2.0 based authentication
- RESTful API for client interactions
- Integration with external services or third parties
- Batch processing of media files,
- Machine learning model to predict optimal number of worker threads,
- Support for file validation and fallback mechanisms,
- Scaling out to additional worker nodes in a cloud environment.

You would need to implement each of these features separately, likely making use of external libraries or services that provide these features, such as cURL (for HTTP requests), Google's OAuth 2.0 library (if you want an off the shelf solution), Docker/Kubernetes for container orchestration, TensorFlow for machine learning models and so on.
