['Here's a basic example of how you could implement this in PHP using Laravel framework, Guzzle for API calls and DB transactions to handle concurrency:

```php
<?php
namespace App\Services;

use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Log;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;
use GuzzleHttp\Client;
use GuzzleHttp\Exception\RequestException;
use Exception;

class ClinicService {
    private $client;

    public function __construct() {
        $this->client = new Client([
            'base_uri' => config('services.api.endpoint'),
        ]);
    }

    public function batchUpdateClinics(array $clinicIds) {
        DB::beginTransaction();

        try{
            foreach ($clinicIds as $id){
                $metadata = $this->fetchMetadataFromAPI($id);

                if (!$metadata) continue;

                DB::table('clinics')
                    ->where('id', $id)
                    ->update([
                        'name' => $metadata['name'],
                        // add other fields to update here
                    ]);
            }

            DB::commit();
        } catch (Exception $e){
            Log::error($e->getMessage());
            DB::rollBack();
            return false;
        }

        return true;
    }

    private function fetchMetadataFromAPI($id) {
        try{
            $response = $this->client->request('GET', '/metadata/' . $id);

            $body = json_decode((string) $response->getBody(), true);

            return $body['data'] ?? null;
        } catch (RequestException $e){
            Log::error("Failed to fetch metadata for clinic id: " . $id . ", Error: ". $e->getMessage());
            // handle error and return null or appropriate value
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```
Note: The implementation is simplified and you'd need to extend it based on your requirements.
The Laravel framework provides a lot of helper classes like DB for database transactions, Log for logging. Guzzle is used for making HTTP requests to the API. Also remember that error handling and input validation in this example are very basic and should be extended according to your needs.
Also, please note you'll need to install and configure Laravel framework, Guzzle and a database driver compatible with your setup (like MySQL or PostgreSQL).
']

